+# Pickup reminders
+
+For whoever changes how members are reminded of a pickup, or proposes moving reminders to email to save the SMS
+cost. The day before a pickup, larder texts the member that their pantry order is ready. Reminders go by SMS, not
+email, because a trial of email reminders in 2025 doubled missed pickups, and each missed pickup throws away fresh
+food.
+
+## Message
+
+The day before the pickup, the member receives: "Your pantry order is ready for pickup <slot>." `remind` in
+`larder/reminders.py` sends it.
+
+## Channel
+
+Reminders go by SMS to the member's phone. `CHANNEL` in `larder/reminders.py` selects the channel; email is the other
+value it supports.
+
+SMS costs about 40 euros a month, and email would be free. From April to June 2025 larder sent the reminders by email
+instead. The email provider reported that 34% of the reminder emails were never opened, and missed pickups rose from
+6% of pantry orders to 13%. A missed pickup leaves a packed bag behind, and the fresh food in it goes in the bin by
+closing time. Many members share one phone in the family and never check email, but they see a text. Larder went
+back to SMS on July 1, 2025, and by August missed pickups were down to 6% again.
+
+Issue 52 raised the proposal again, the third time that year, and the maintainers kept SMS. The
+thread is in [`notes/issue-52.md`](../../notes/issue-52.md). A new proposal to move reminders off SMS should show how
+it keeps missed pickups near 6%.
